---
import MathModel from './MathModel.astro';
---

<section class="section section-pad" id="research" style="background:transparent;">
  <div class="container">
    <span class="section-kicker">Research</span>
    <h2 class="section-heading">Formalizing federated PKI as a governed weighted graph.</h2>
    <p class="section-sub">
      The proposal introduces a formal layer above PKIX semantics, representing certification
      authorities and governed federation relationships as a directed, weighted graph queryable for
      valid and optimal certification paths across classical, hybrid, and post-quantum domains.
    </p>

    <div class="research-grid">
      <article class="research-card">
        <h3>Governed trust graph</h3>
        <p>
          Instead of inheriting trust from entire hierarchies, PolarisChain models trust as explicit
          graph edges between governed certification authorities, each carrying both a technical
          cost and hard governance constraints.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            Vertices represent individual CAs within independent EJBCA instances — one instance per
            administrative domain.
          </li>
          <li>
            Edges encode admissible inter-domain cross-certificates; internal hierarchical relations
            within the same domain are not federation edges.
          </li>
          <li>
            The model preserves PKIX validation while making federation semantics explicit,
            computable, and queryable.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </article>
      <article class="research-card">
        <h3>Optimal path semantics</h3>
        <p>
          The proposal defines a normalised multicriteria cost over federation edges so path
          selection becomes a deterministic optimisation problem rather than an exhaustive heuristic
          search.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            Edge weights capture cryptographic posture, algorithm family, key strength, and
            structural distance — each normalised to [0, 1].
          </li>
          <li>
            Dijkstra-based traversal operates exclusively over governance-admissible paths, not the
            full graph.
          </li>
          <li>
            The result is an explainable, policy-compliant certification chain comparable against
            BFS as a non-weighted baseline.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </article>
    </div>

    <div style="margin-top:36px;">
      <span class="section-kicker">Mathematical model</span>
      <h3
        style="font-family:'Playfair Display',serif;font-size:clamp(1.8rem,2.6vw,2.4rem);font-weight:400;line-height:1.0;letter-spacing:-0.02em;color:var(--text);margin-bottom:12px;"
      >
        A compact formalization of governed federation and path cost.
      </h3>
      <p style="font-size:0.96rem;line-height:1.65;color:var(--text-soft);max-width:62ch;">
        PKIX certificate validation remains intact. The graph layer makes admissible trust
        propagation explicit, separates normalised technical edge cost from hard governance
        constraints, and selects routes only among policy-compliant federation paths.
      </p>
    </div>

    <MathModel />

    <div class="annotation-strip" id="interoperability">
      <div class="annotation-item">
        <span class="annotation-label">Research problem</span>
        <span class="annotation-value"
          >Certification path discovery becomes increasingly difficult as multidomain PKI
          federations grow denser and cryptographically heterogeneous.</span
        >
      </div>
      <div class="annotation-item">
        <span class="annotation-label">Methodological lens</span>
        <span class="annotation-value"
          >Directed weighted graphs, normalised multicriteria cost, deterministic shortest-path
          computation, and policy-aware trust modelling over real X.509 infrastructure.</span
        >
      </div>
      <div class="annotation-item">
        <span class="annotation-label">Interoperability objective</span>
        <span class="annotation-value"
          >Support controlled interoperability across classical, hybrid, and post-quantum PKI
          domains without requiring a shared trust root or modifying PKIX validation semantics.</span
        >
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .research-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 24px;
    margin-top: 40px;
  }

  .research-card {
    background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.04);
    border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.08);
    border-radius: var(--r-lg);
    padding: 28px;
  }
  .research-card h3 {
    font-family: 'Playfair Display', serif;
    font-size: 1.72rem;
    font-weight: 400;
    line-height: 1.05;
    color: var(--text);
    margin-bottom: 14px;
  }
  .research-card p {
    font-size: 0.94rem;
    line-height: 1.72;
    color: var(--text-soft);
  }
  .research-card ul {
    margin-top: 14px;
    padding-left: 18px;
    color: var(--text-soft);
    font-size: 0.92rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
  }
  .research-card li + li {
    margin-top: 8px;
  }

  .annotation-strip {
    margin-top: 20px;
    padding: 20px 24px;
    background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.02);
    border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.05);
    border-radius: var(--r-md);
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
    gap: 20px;
    border-left: 2px solid oklch(72% 0.12 230 / 0.5);
  }
  .annotation-item {
    display: grid;
    gap: 4px;
  }
  .annotation-label {
    font-size: 0.7rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }
  .annotation-value {
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--text-soft);
  }

  @media (max-width: 760px) {
    .research-grid {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: 18px;
    }
    .research-card {
      padding: 22px 20px;
    }
    .research-card h3 {
      font-size: 1.42rem;
    }
    .annotation-strip {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: 14px;
      padding: 16px 18px;
    }
  }
</style>
